  "worked_solution": "For a binomial random variable $X\u223cB(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X\u223cB(7,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 3)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=3,4,5,6,7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X<3)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X<3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}+7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}+21{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & \u22480.227\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & \u22481-0.227 \\\\  & =0.773\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
